<section class="page-head">
  <h1>Build your own MCP</h1>
  <p class="section-note">I wrote about it, so I figured I should build one. A tiny, working MCP server — download it and add it to your favorite AI tool.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study">
  <p class="lede">What it does: it hands your AI two small tools — roll some dice, and pull up a quick bio of me. Nothing mission-critical; the point is the pattern, not the party tricks.</p>
  <h2>Why</h2>
  <p>
    I wrote a post — <a href="/posts/you-down-with-mcp/">You down with MCP?</a> — arguing that MCP is
    the boring plumbing that makes the interesting agent stuff possible. Writing it left me with an
    itch: if this protocol is so foundational, how hard is it to actually build one myself? Turns out,
    not hard at all. So I built a tiny one to prove the point.
  </p>

  <h2>The gist</h2>
  <ul class="case-steps">
    <li>Pick what to expose — a <em>tool</em> (an action) or a <em>resource</em> (data). Start with one.</li>
    <li>Install the official SDK: <code>pip install mcp</code> (Python; there's a TypeScript one too).</li>
    <li>Define each tool: a name, a description, typed inputs, and a handler function.</li>
    <li>Pick a transport — <code>stdio</code> for local is the easy default.</li>
    <li>Point your MCP client (Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor…) at the file and restart.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No LLM API key needed to <em>run</em> the server — it just exposes tools. Your client brings the model.</p>

  <h2>The whole server</h2>
  <p>That's not a teaser — this is the entire thing:</p>
  <pre class="codeblock"><code>from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP
import random

mcp = FastMCP("ryan-bowlin-starter")

@mcp.tool()
def roll_dice(sides: int = 6, count: int = 1) -> str:
    """Roll `count` dice with `sides` sides each; returns the rolls and total."""
    rolls = [random.randint(1, sides) for _ in range(count)]
    return f"Rolled {count}d{sides}: {rolls}  (total: {sum(rolls)})"

@mcp.tool()
def about_ryan() -> str:
    """A short bio for Ryan Bowlin and where to find his work."""
    return "Ryan Bowlin — Senior PM who builds and ships production AI. https://rcbowlin1.github.io"

if __name__ == "__main__":
    mcp.run()  # stdio transport by default</code></pre>

  <p class="hero-actions"><a class="btn" href="/ryan-bowlin-mcp.py" download>Download the server (.py) ↓</a></p>

  <h2>Add it to your tool</h2>
  <p>Drop this into your MCP client's config (paths and file name may vary by client), then restart:</p>
  <pre class="codeblock"><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "ryan-bowlin-starter": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": ["/full/path/to/ryan-bowlin-mcp.py"]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
  <p>Restart the client and your agent can now roll dice and, apparently, tell you about me. Swap in your own tools from there — the pattern is the whole point.</p>

  <h2>The takeaway</h2>
  <p>
    ~30 lines, and any agent that speaks MCP can now use it. That's the argument from the post made
    concrete: the protocol is deliberately boring, which is exactly why it scales.
  </p>

  <p class="back">Want the <em>why</em> behind this — where MCP came from and why it matters? Read <a href="/posts/you-down-with-mcp/">You down with MCP? Yeah, you know me!</a></p>
</section>
